<article class="env-lemma" id="0C73">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0C73">Lemma <span data-tag="0C73">55.3.5</span>.</a> Let $n, m_ i, a_{ij}, w_ i, g_ i$ be a numerical type of genus $g$. If $n = 1$, then $a_{11} = 0$ and $g = 1 + m_1w_1(g_1 - 1)$. Moreover, we can classify all such numerical types as follows </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $g < 0$, then $g_1 = 0$ and there are finitely many possible numerical types of genus $g$ with $n = 1$ corresponding to factorizations $m_1w_1 = 1 - g$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $g = 0$, then $m_1 = 1$, $w_1 = 1$, $g_1 = 0$ as in Lemma <a href="/tag/0C8S" data-tag="0C8S">55.6.1</a>. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $g = 1$, then we conclude $g_1 = 1$ but $m_1, w_1$ can be arbitrary positive integers; this is case (<a href="/tag/0C8U" data-tag="0C8U">1</a>) of Lemma <a href="/tag/0C8T" data-tag="0C8T">55.6.2</a>. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $g > 1$, then $g_1 > 1$ and there are finitely many possible numerical types of genus $g$ with $n = 1$ corresponding to factorizations $m_1w_1(g_1 - 1) = g - 1$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The lemma proves itself. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>